MC-DC (Monte Carlo Diffusion and Collision) Simulator
Overview
The MC-DC Simulator is an advanced Diffusion MRI (dMRI) simulation tool that models the diffusion of particles in complex microstructures. It's designed to simulate and analyze diffusion-weighted MRI signals in biological tissues, particularly useful for studying brain microstructure.
Key Features
Particle Dynamics Simulation
Simulates the random walk of particles (spins) in complex microstructures
Supports both free diffusion and restricted diffusion scenarios
Handles collisions with various types of obstacles (spheres, cylinders, PLY meshes)
Multiple Obstacle Types
Spheres
Cylinders
PLY mesh models
Voxel-based boundaries
Support for gamma-distributed obstacles
Signal Synthesis
Supports PGSE (Pulsed Gradient Spin Echo) sequences
Can compute both real and imaginary parts of the DWI signal
Handles separate intra- and extra-axonal signals
Parallel Processing
Multi-threaded simulation capabilities
Efficient distribution of particles across available processors
AABB (Axis-Aligned Bounding Box) grid optimization for collision detection
Recent Changes and Improvements
Performance Optimizations
Implementation of AABB grids for faster collision detection
Improved parallel processing capabilities
Enhanced memory management for large-scale simulations
New Features
Support for gradient waveforms
Enhanced PLY mesh obstacle handling
Improved signal synthesis capabilities
Better handling of intra- and extra-axonal compartments
Code Structure Improvements
Better organization of simulation components
Enhanced error handling and parameter validation
Improved configuration file handling
Important Files
Core Simulation Files
dynamicsSimulation.cpp/h: Main implementation of particle dynamics and collision handling
mcsimulation.cpp/h: Core simulation class managing the overall simulation process
parallelmcsimulation.cpp/h: Handles parallel processing and multi-threading
Signal Processing
simulablesequence.h: Base class for MRI sequence simulation
pgsesequence.cpp/h: Implementation of PGSE sequence simulation
gradientwaveform.cpp/h: Support for custom gradient waveforms
Obstacle Handling
sphere.cpp/h: Sphere obstacle implementation
cylinder.cpp/h: Cylinder obstacle implementation
plyobstacle.cpp/h: PLY mesh obstacle handling
Utility and Configuration
simerrno.cpp/h: Error handling and parameter validation
parameters.cpp/h: Configuration parameter management
constants.h: Global constants and definitions


Now, what we are doing now is to implement several changes to the core funtionality.

We have succesfully implemented several new features like t2 relaxation, permeable models, bounding box based voxels, and better initializaton and volume computation.



Missing features and tasks (Roadmap): 

1) 
Our next task is to implement symmetric boundary conditions by including in the walker's class a vector that saves when the particle real position needs to be 
mirrored because it touched the bounderies. 

Then we will handle the colision of the particle to the voxel as a normal elastic bounsing when it's symetic boundaries, or we will jsut map it back to the other side when it's 
periodic boundaries. 


2) Secodn task is to verify that all the inputs from the .conf file are now standarized to standard units SU, and then proper ly scaled to the simualtor scale mT, mm, ms.


3) We will improve the PLY class to read fiels that contains also faces colors (we will use it as a label if it's intra, extra, or other) and 
also the funtion to read binary ply format. 

5) Python wrapper with funtions to create and call simulations more easily, visualize outputs, parameters and even the protocol. 


